\title{Affine Zero-Knowledge Protocols\\for Hidden Mathematical Witnesses}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 17, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study a three-move protocol for proving knowledge of a preimage under a homomorphism of finite abelian groups. Given finite abelian groups $W,V$, a homomorphism $L:W\to V$, and public $y\in V$, the prover claims knowledge of $w\in W$ with $L(w)=y$. The prover commits with $L(r)$ for a uniform mask $r$, receives a Boolean challenge $e$, and answers with $r+ew$. We prove perfect completeness, exact honest-verifier zero knowledge for each fixed challenge, witness independence of transcript distributions, and special soundness: opposite accepting responses with a common commitment reveal a witness by subtraction. Explicit simulation and extraction algorithms and cyclic examples are given. We also explain why these algebraic results do not alone establish succinct zero-knowledge certification for arbitrary mathematical theorems. Such an extension requires sound locally testable encodings, hiding and binding commitments, an adversarial probability model, and explicit complexity accounting.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A mathematical proof usually establishes truth and communicates a reason at once. Zero knowledge separates these functions: a prover may establish possession of a witness while disclosing no information about it beyond the public statement. This suggests confidential certification of derivations, designs, or optimization solutions. The global ambition, however, should not obscure the local obligations. Opening one randomly selected line of a committed derivation does not prove that the whole derivation is correct, and encoding a proof as an integer does not make its bit representation short.

We isolate an exact algebraic core. A public statement is a homomorphic image $y=L(w)$. Random translation makes a challenged response uniform and witness-independent. Two answers to opposite challenges under one commitment cancel the mask and reveal a witness. The construction yields four results: perfect completeness, perfect fixed-challenge simulation, witness independence, and special soundness. The first three are information-theoretic; the fourth is an algebraic extraction guarantee.

\section{Algebraic setting and protocol}

\begin{definition}[Public relation]
Let $W$ and $V$ be finite abelian groups, written additively, and let $L:W\to V$ be a homomorphism. For public $y\in V$, a witness is $w\in W$ satisfying $L(w)=y$. The relation is
\[
R_L=\{(y,w)\in V\times W:L(w)=y\}.
\]
No injectivity or surjectivity assumption is imposed.
\end{definition}

For a valid witness $w$, the protocol is:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
\item The prover samples $r\in W$ uniformly and sends $t=L(r)$.
\item The verifier sends $e\in\{0,1\}$.
\item The prover sends $z=r+ew$.
\end{enumerate}
The verifier accepts precisely when
\[
L(z)=t+ey.
\]
A transcript is $(t,e,z)\in V\times\{0,1\}\times W$. For fixed $e$, the real transcript map is
\[
\Real_{w,e}(r)=(L(r),e,r+ew).
\]

\begin{definition}[Security notions]
Perfect completeness means that every honest transcript from a valid witness is accepted. Perfect honest-verifier zero knowledge for fixed $e$ means that a simulator using only $(L,y,e)$ has an output distribution exactly equal to the real distribution. Special soundness means that two accepting transcripts $(t,0,z_0)$ and $(t,1,z_1)$ yield a witness efficiently.
\end{definition}

The fixed-challenge qualification is important: these notions do not yet quantify over arbitrary adaptive verifier strategies.

\section{Completeness and perfect simulation}

\begin{theorem}[Perfect Completeness]
If $L(w)=y$, then for every $r\in W$ and $e\in\{0,1\}$, the transcript $(L(r),e,r+ew)$ is accepted.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By the homomorphism law and the witness equation,
\[
L(r+ew)=L(r)+eL(w)=L(r)+ey.
\]
This is the verification equation with $t=L(r)$.
\end{proof}

Fix $e$. Define a public simulator by sampling $z\in W$ uniformly, setting
\[
t=L(z)-ey,
\]
and outputting $(t,e,z)$. Every such transcript accepts because $L(z)=t+ey$.

\begin{lemma}[Translation Preserves Uniformity]
For fixed $a\in W$, if $r$ is uniform on $W$, then $r+a$ is uniform on $W$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Translation $r\mapsto r+a$ is a permutation with inverse $z\mapsto z-a$. A permutation of a finite set preserves uniform probabilities.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Perfect Fixed-Challenge Simulation]
Let $L(w)=y$ and fix $e\in\{0,1\}$. The real transcript
\[
(L(r),e,r+ew),\qquad r\leftarrow W,
\]
has exactly the same distribution as
\[
(L(z)-ey,e,z),\qquad z\leftarrow W.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Set $z=r+ew$. Translation preserves uniformity. Moreover,
\[
L(r)=L(z-ew)=L(z)-eL(w)=L(z)-ey.
\]
After this bijective change of variables, the real transcript is pointwise the simulator transcript. Thus all transcript multiplicities, and therefore all probabilities, agree.
\end{proof}

The result concerns the joint distribution: although commitment and response are correlated, the simulator reproduces their correlation exactly.

\begin{corollary}[Witness Independence]
If $L(w_0)=L(w_1)=y$, then for each fixed challenge $e$, witnesses $w_0$ and $w_1$ induce identical transcript distributions.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Both distributions equal the same simulator distribution, which depends only on public data and $e$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Information-Theoretic Privacy]
For fixed $e$, every statistical test has the same output distribution on real and simulated transcripts. No observer, even computationally unbounded, has a nonzero distinguishing advantage.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Identically distributed inputs remain identically distributed under arbitrary post-processing.
\end{proof}

When $L$ has a nontrivial kernel, the witness fiber contains $w+k$ for every $k\in\ker L$. Witness independence says that a transcript cannot identify the representative used.

\section{Special soundness}

\begin{theorem}[Special Soundness by Affine Subtraction]
Suppose $(t,0,z_0)$ and $(t,1,z_1)$ are accepting transcripts with the same commitment. Then
\[
w^*=z_1-z_0
\]
is a witness: $L(w^*)=y$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Acceptance gives $L(z_0)=t$ and $L(z_1)=t+y$. Subtracting,
\[
L(z_1-z_0)=L(z_1)-L(z_0)=(t+y)-t=y.
\]
No assumption about honest response generation is needed.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Recovery from Honest Responses]
If $z_0=r$ and $z_1=r+w$, then extraction returns the original witness $w$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
$(r+w)-r=w$.
\end{proof}

The common commitment is essential. With commitments $t_0,t_1$, subtraction gives $L(z_1-z_0)=y+(t_1-t_0)$, which need not equal $y$. Applications must therefore fix the commitment before the challenge and prevent challenge-dependent replacement.

If a witnessless prover can answer at most one challenge for a fixed commitment, one uniform challenge is guessed with probability at most $1/2$. Under independent repetition, binding commitments, and an explicit adversarial model, $k$ guesses succeed with probability at most $2^{-k}$. The algebra supplies the local extraction fact; independence and binding supply the probability theorem.

\section{Cyclic example}

Take $W=V=\Z/q\Z$ and $L(x)=ax\pmod q$. The prover knows $w$ with $y=aw\pmod q$, samples $r$, sends $t=ar$, and answers $z=r+ew$. Verification is
\[
az\equiv t+ey\pmod q.
\]
The simulator samples $z$ and sets $t=az-ey\pmod q$.

\begin{example}
Let $q=12$, $a=4$, and $y=8$. The witnesses are $2,5,8,11$. Every one produces the same distribution for either fixed challenge. Choose $w=5$ and $r=7$. Then $t=4$, $z_0=7$, and $z_1=0$. We have
\[
4z_0\equiv4=t,\qquad 4z_1\equiv0\equiv4+8=t+y\pmod{12}.
\]
Extraction returns $z_1-z_0\equiv5\pmod{12}$, a witness.
\end{example}

This example also shows that injectivity is unnecessary. Exact simulation remains valid for a noninvertible multiplier and a public statement with several witnesses.

\section{Algorithms and complexity}

\paragraph{Real generation.} Sample uniform $r$, compute $t=L(r)$ and $z=r+ew$, and return $(t,e,z)$. If one group operation costs $C_W$ and evaluation of $L$ costs $C_L$, the cost beyond sampling is $O(C_L+C_W)$.

\paragraph{Public simulation.} Sample uniform $z$, compute $t=L(z)-ey$, and return $(t,e,z)$. It has the same asymptotic cost and neither searches for a witness nor inverts $L$.

\paragraph{Extraction.} Given accepting $(t,0,z_0)$ and $(t,1,z_1)$, verify the shared commitment and equations, then return $z_1-z_0$. Extraction itself is one group subtraction; including checks costs two evaluations of $L$ and a constant number of operations.

\paragraph{Finite exhaustive comparison.} For $N=|W|$, enumerate all real masks and all simulated responses, count transcripts, and compare frequency maps. Hash-table comparison takes expected $O(N)$ time and $O(N)$ space. This is useful for demonstration, while the simulation theorem applies without exhaustive computation.

\section{From witnesses to hidden mathematical proofs}

Fix a deductive theory with decidable inference rules. A derivation of $T$ is finite data
\[
\pi=(\varphi_1,\ldots,\varphi_m),
\]
where each line is an axiom or follows from cited earlier lines, and $\varphi_m=T$. Hence validity is a decidable relation $R(T,\pi)$. A derivation can therefore serve as a cryptographic witness.

This observation does not turn general proof verification into a homomorphic preimage relation. Nor does committing to every line and opening one random line prove global validity. If only one of $m$ lines is defective, one uniform query detects it with probability $1/m$. A sound locally testable encoding must transform global invalidity into many detectable local inconsistencies.

Four obligations must be distinguished:
\begin{itemize}
\item local soundness, which makes false encodings detectably inconsistent;
\item zero knowledge, which hides information in queried views;
\item binding, which prevents post-challenge changes;
\item succinctness, which bounds communication and verifier work.
\end{itemize}
None automatically follows from another.

A claim of communication polynomial only in statement length is particularly strong. Arithmetizing a proof does not compress its binary representation, and a local-checking transformation may remain polynomial in witness length. A meaningful complexity theorem must state formula encoding, statement length, proof length, security parameter, prover and verifier time, randomness, and total communication. Genuinely succinct argument machinery is needed to detach communication from proof length.

Finally, fixed-challenge honest-verifier simulation is not malicious-verifier zero knowledge. The latter quantifies over arbitrary verifier strategies and simulates their complete adaptive views, potentially using rewinding or a different protocol compilation.

\section{Applications and limitations}

The construction directly supports privacy-preserving identification for finite linear relations, including witness anonymity when $L$ has a nontrivial kernel. It also supplies a modular protocol component that larger systems may combine with commitments, repetition, and malicious-verifier transformations.

More broadly, engineering designs, optimization solutions, and mathematical derivations can all be witnesses to public predicates. The present affine relation does not implement all such predicates, but it exposes the necessary architecture: simulated views, binding commitments, global soundness, and explicit complexity bounds.

Confidential certification also has a conceptual limitation. It may establish confidence without communicating explanation. In mathematics, proofs reveal structure and generate reusable ideas. A hidden certificate complements exposition; it does not replace it.

\section{Future work}

The first extension is to formulate transcript equality directly with probability mass functions and prove preservation under products. This supports sequential and parallel repetition.

A complete repetition theorem should model adversarial commitments, independence, and binding explicitly. Under suitable assumptions, the target soundness error decreases exponentially with the number of rounds.

A further direction is a locally checkable encoding of propositional derivations with separate theorems for global soundness, privacy of openings, and commitment security. Randomly opening unencoded proof lines is insufficient.

Concrete cyclic instantiations should also be related to identification protocols written multiplicatively, carefully separating additive witness notation from exponentiation in a public group.

For confidential theorem certification, algorithms and bit lengths must be represented explicitly. Any arithmetic-wide succinctness claim must account for proof length and rely on genuinely succinct arguments rather than arithmetization alone. Finally, malicious-verifier simulation should cover arbitrary strategies, complete views, expected running time, and any rewinding procedure.

\section{Conclusion}

For homomorphic preimage relations over finite abelian groups, the affine protocol has an exact dual character. Translation by a uniform mask gives perfect fixed-challenge privacy and witness-independent transcript distributions. Subtraction of opposite accepting responses under a common commitment extracts a witness. These facts are complete at the algebraic level and sharply delimit the additional work needed for universal confidential theorem certification: local testability, commitment security, adversarial simulation, repetition, and complexity accounting.

\end{document}

